### AutoParts ERP - sample requests
### Install the "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client) and click "Send Request" above any block.

@host = https://localhost:7150
@tenant = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001

### Service info: which modules are loaded
GET {{host}}/

### Health
GET {{host}}/health

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reference data
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Brands, with a count of the parts carrying each
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/brands
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Categories
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/categories
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Create a brand
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/brands
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "code": "TRW",
  "name": "TRW Automotive",
  "isOriginalEquipment": false,
  "countryCode": "DE"
}

### Create a category
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/categories
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "code": "BRK-DSC",
  "name": "Brake Discs",
  "sortOrder": 20
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The searches the business actually runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Everything recorded as fitting a 2015 Golf VII
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/for-vehicle?make=Volkswagen&model=Golf VII&year=2015
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Narrowed by engine code - far fewer wrong-fit returns
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/for-vehicle?make=Volkswagen&model=Golf VII&year=2015&engineCode=CJZA
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### An OEM number read off the old part, typed with spaces
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts?term=5Q0 698 151 A
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### The same number without them. Same result - that is the point.
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts?term=5q0698151a
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### A few words from the description
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts?term=brake pad
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Only parts sold against a returnable core
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts?requiresCoreReturn=true
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### One part by SKU, the way a counter terminal looks it up
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/by-sku/BP-1188
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setting up a new part
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1. Create it. Copy a brandId and categoryId from the list calls above.
# @name createPart
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/parts
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "sku": "BD-3301",
  "manufacturerPartNumber": "DF 4249",
  "brandId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-BRAND-ID",
  "categoryId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-CATEGORY-ID",
  "name": "Brake disc, front axle, vented",
  "stockUnitCode": "EA",
  "description": "Vented front brake disc, 312mm."
}

@partId = {{createPart.response.body.$}}

### 2. Link the OEM number a customer will quote
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/{{partId}}/cross-references
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "kind": "Oem",
  "number": "5Q0 615 301 F",
  "sourceBrand": "VW",
  "notes": "312mm vented"
}

### 3. Record what it fits
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/{{partId}}/fitments
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "make": "Volkswagen",
  "model": "Golf VII",
  "yearFrom": 2012,
  "yearTo": 2020,
  "engineCode": "CJZA",
  "position": "FRONT"
}

### 4. Make it orderable and sellable
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/{{partId}}/activate
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 5. Read it back in full
GET {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/{{partId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules the API will refuse - each returns a stable errorCode
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Duplicate SKU -> 409 catalog.part.sku_exists
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/parts
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "sku": "BP-1188",
  "manufacturerPartNumber": "99 999 99",
  "brandId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-BRAND-ID",
  "categoryId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-CATEGORY-ID",
  "name": "Duplicate SKU test",
  "stockUnitCode": "EA"
}

### Activating an already-discontinued part -> 422 catalog.part.cannot_reactivate
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/parts/{{partId}}/discontinue
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{}

### Missing required fields -> 400 with every bad field listed at once
POST {{host}}/api/catalog/parts
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "sku": "",
  "manufacturerPartNumber": "",
  "brandId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
  "categoryId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
  "name": "",
  "stockUnitCode": "PARSEC"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purchasing
#
# The full loop, in order. Steps 1-2 need a supplier and a part you already
# have: grab them from /api/partners?isSupplier=true and /api/catalog/parts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1. Start a draft order. The supplier's code is read from Partners, not typed.
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "supplierId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-SUPPLIER-PARTNER-ID",
  "warehouseId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-WAREHOUSE-ID",
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "notes": "Weekly stock order."
}

### 2. Put a part on it. The SKU, the description and the unit come from the catalogue -
### only the part, the quantity and the agreed price are typed. A part that is not
### purchasable is refused here with purchasing.line.part_not_purchasable.
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-PART-ID",
  "quantity": 10,
  "unitPrice": 24.50
}

### 3. Send it to the supplier. This is the point of commitment.
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/submit
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "expectedOn": "2026-09-15" }

### 4. Record their acknowledgement.
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/confirm
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "expectedOn": "2026-09-16", "supplierReference": "ACK-77213" }

### 5. Book part of the delivery in. Inventory picks this up and adds the stock.
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/lines/{{lineId}}/receipts
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "quantity": 6 }

### 6. Check the stock actually moved - on hand should have gone up by 6.
GET {{host}}/api/inventory/stock?partId={{partId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### The order now reads PartiallyReceived, with 4 still outstanding
GET {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### The buyer's working list
GET {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders?outstandingOnly=true
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Orders past their promised date
GET {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders?overdueOnly=true
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Look one up the way you read it off a delivery note
GET {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/by-number/PO-2026-00001
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Replenishment
#
# Nothing creates these by hand. Issue stock in Inventory until a part drops
# below its reorder point and one appears here on its own.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Parts that have run low, worst shortfall first
GET {{host}}/api/purchasing/suggestions
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Roll one onto a draft order and mark it dealt with
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/suggestions/{{suggestionId}}/order-line
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "purchaseOrderId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-DRAFT-ORDER-ID",
  "unitPrice": 24.50
}

### Or decide not to buy it, with a reason
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/suggestions/{{suggestionId}}/dismiss
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "reason": "Being run down; sell the shelf stock first." }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules Purchasing will refuse
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Receiving more than was ordered -> 422 purchasing.line.over_receipt
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/lines/{{lineId}}/receipts
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "quantity": 9999 }

### Editing an order that has gone out -> 422 purchasing.order.not_editable
DELETE {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/lines/{{lineId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Cancelling after goods arrived -> 422 purchasing.order.cannot_cancel_after_receipt
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/cancel
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "reason": "Changed our mind." }

### Accepting the shortfall instead -> 204
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders/{{orderId}}/close-short
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "reason": "Supplier had no more; agreed to leave it." }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sales
#
# Customer accounts are NOT created here. They arrive from Partners: grant the
# customer role to a partner and the account appears within a few seconds,
# once the outbox sweep has delivered the event.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Who can we sell to, and how much credit is left
GET {{host}}/api/sales/customers
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### One account, by the code typed at the counter
GET {{host}}/api/sales/customers/by-code/WKSP
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 1. Start an order. Code, name and currency come off the account.
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "customerId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-CUSTOMER-PARTNER-ID",
  "warehouseId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-WAREHOUSE-ID",
  "kind": "Order",
  "customerReference": "PO-4471",
  "notes": "Collecting Friday."
}

### 2. Add a line. 10 at 24.50, 10% off, 23% VAT -> net 220.50, VAT 50.72, gross 271.22
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-PART-ID",
  "quantity": 10
}

### Same line with the price set by hand. The line records that no price list was behind it.
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-PART-ID",
  "quantity": 10,
  "unitPrice": 18.00
}

### Keeping the quoted price but overriding the discount
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-PART-ID",
  "quantity": 10,
  "discountPercent": 12
}

### A part no price list mentions -> 404 sales.line.no_price
### Pricing's own /quote endpoint says which of the several reasons it is.

### A part priced in another currency -> 422 sales.line.price_currency_mismatch.
### Refused, never converted.

### 3. Confirm. Checks stock, then the hold and the credit, then claims the stock.
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/confirm
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "requiredBy": "2026-09-10" }

### 4. Check Inventory held it - reserved should have gone up by 10, on hand unchanged
GET {{host}}/api/inventory/stock?partId={{partId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 5. Ship four of the ten. The claim is released, 4 issued, 6 re-claimed.
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/lines/{{lineId}}/dispatches
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "quantity": 4 }

### 6. On hand down by 4, reserved down to 6
GET {{host}}/api/inventory/stock?partId={{partId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### The credit committed against the account until the order ships in full
GET {{host}}/api/sales/customers/{{customerId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### The picking list
GET {{host}}/api/sales/orders?outstandingOnly=true
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Orders past their promised date
GET {{host}}/api/sales/orders?lateOnly=true
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules Sales will refuse
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Put the partner on hold in Partners first...
POST {{host}}/api/partners/{{customerId}}/hold
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "reason": "Invoice 4471 is 40 days overdue." }

### ...then this comes back 422 sales.customer.on_hold, a few seconds later
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "customerId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-CUSTOMER-PARTNER-ID",
  "warehouseId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-WAREHOUSE-ID",
  "kind": "Order"
}

### An order beyond the limit -> 422 sales.customer.credit_limit_exceeded
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/confirm
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{}

### Shipping more than was sold -> 422 sales.line.over_dispatch
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/lines/{{lineId}}/dispatches
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "quantity": 9999 }

### Cancelling after goods went out -> 422 sales.order.cannot_cancel_after_dispatch
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/cancel
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "reason": "Customer changed their mind." }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Module query contracts
#
# The two places one module now asks another a question synchronously,
# instead of trusting the caller or finding out an hour later in a log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Confirming without enough stock -> 422 sales.line.insufficient_stock, with the numbers
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/confirm
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{}

### The same order, taken deliberately as a back-order -> 204
POST {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{salesOrderId}}/confirm
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "allowBackorder": true }

### Raising a purchase order on a partner who is not a supplier
### -> 422 purchasing.order.supplier_not_purchasable
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "supplierId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-CUSTOMER-ONLY-PARTNER-ID",
  "warehouseId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-WAREHOUSE-ID",
  "currencyCode": "EUR"
}

### ...or on a partner who does not exist -> 404 purchasing.order.supplier_not_found
POST {{host}}/api/purchasing/orders
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "supplierId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000099",
  "warehouseId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-WAREHOUSE-ID",
  "currencyCode": "EUR"
}

### Put a supplier on hold in Partners, then try to buy from them
### -> 422 purchasing.order.supplier_not_purchasable, immediately (no outbox delay)
POST {{host}}/api/partners/{{supplierId}}/hold
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "reason": "Quality dispute on the last three deliveries." }


###############################################################################
# Pricing
#
# The order matters here: a list opens in draft, gets prices, then goes live.
# Quoting from a draft list gets you nothing, deliberately.
###############################################################################

### 1. Open the standard list. This is what a walk-in customer pays.
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/lists
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "code": "TRADE",
  "name": "Trade list 2026",
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "kind": "Standard"
}

### Same code twice -> 409 pricing.list.code_exists
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/lists
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "code": "trade",
  "name": "Duplicate",
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "kind": "Standard"
}

### A promotion with no last day -> 422 pricing.list.promotion_needs_end
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/lists
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "code": "FOREVER",
  "name": "Permanent sale",
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "kind": "Promotion"
}

### 2. Price a part. One unit is EUR 24.50.
PUT {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/prices/{{partId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "minimumQuantity": 1, "unitPrice": 24.50 }

### 3. Ten or more is EUR 22.00. Same endpoint - it adds the break.
PUT {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/prices/{{partId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "minimumQuantity": 10, "unitPrice": 22.00 }

### 4. Fifty or more is EUR 20.00.
PUT {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/prices/{{partId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "minimumQuantity": 50, "unitPrice": 20.00 }

### Correcting the ten-up price. Same call again - not a conflict.
PUT {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/prices/{{partId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "minimumQuantity": 10, "unitPrice": 21.50 }

### What the list now says about that part
GET {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/prices/{{partId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 5. Put the list into service.
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/activate
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 6. Make it the fallback for customers with no agreement.
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/make-default
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Quote one. Walk-in, no account -> 24.50
GET {{host}}/api/pricing/quote?partId={{partId}}&quantity=1
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Quote ten -> 21.50, appliedBreakQuantity 10
GET {{host}}/api/pricing/quote?partId={{partId}}&quantity=10
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Quote fifty -> 20.00, appliedBreakQuantity 50
GET {{host}}/api/pricing/quote?partId={{partId}}&quantity=50
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 7. A customer on 5%. Quote fifty for them -> gross 20.00, net 19.00.
### The discount comes off AFTER the break, which is what everybody assumes.
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/agreements
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "customerId": "REPLACE-WITH-A-CUSTOMER-ID",
  "priceListId": "{{priceListId}}",
  "discountPercent": 5,
  "note": "Loyal since 2019."
}

GET {{host}}/api/pricing/quote?partId={{partId}}&quantity=50&customerId={{customerId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Same customer twice -> 409 pricing.agreement.already_agreed
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/agreements
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{ "customerId": "{{customerId}}", "priceListId": "{{priceListId}}", "discountPercent": 10 }

### Who would a change to this list reach?
GET {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/agreements
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### End their terms. They go back to the default list at full price.
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/agreements/{{customerId}}/end
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{}

### 8. A promotion that beats everything while it runs.
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/lists
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "code": "SEP26",
  "name": "September brakes campaign",
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "kind": "Promotion",
  "effectiveFrom": "2026-09-01",
  "effectiveTo": "2026-09-30"
}

### Price the same part on it, activate it, and quote again.
### The promotion wins because it is a promotion - not because it is cheaper.

### Asking for a currency the list does not price in
### -> 422 pricing.quote.currency_mismatch. Refused, never converted.
GET {{host}}/api/pricing/quote?partId={{partId}}&quantity=1&currencyCode=USD
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### A part nobody has priced -> 404 pricing.quote.no_price
GET {{host}}/api/pricing/quote?partId=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000099&quantity=1
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### The default list cannot be withdrawn while it is the default
### -> 422 pricing.list.cannot_archive_default
POST {{host}}/api/pricing/lists/{{priceListId}}/archive
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

###############################################################################
# INVOICING
#
# The order below is the only order that works, and each step refuses until the
# one before it has happened. That is the law rather than a design choice: a
# series has to be declared to the tax authority and given a validation code
# before it can number anything, and a document has to be complete before it
# can take a number, because a number taken and not used is a gap in a sequence
# that is required to have none.
###############################################################################

@seriesId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@invoiceId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### 1. Open a series. It exists, and it can issue nothing.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/series
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "type": "FT",
  "code": "SERIE2026",
  "year": 2026
}

### Try to issue in it now -> 422 invoicing.series.not_validated.
### Worth doing once, to see the refusal before the code is recorded.

### 2. Record the code the AT returned. Once, and never again.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/series/{{seriesId}}/validation-code
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "validationCode": "CSDF7T5H"
}

### Sending a second code -> 422 invoicing.series.already_validated.
### It is in every ATCUD the series has produced, so it cannot move.

### 3. Put it into service.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/series/{{seriesId}}/activate
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 4. See where it stands: status, next number, how many it has issued.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/series/{{seriesId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 5. Start a document. A draft: no number, no ATCUD, no legal standing.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "type": "FT",
  "customerId": "{{customerId}}",
  "customerName": "Oficina Central, Lda.",
  "customerTaxNumber": "501234567",
  "customerCountry": "PT",
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "documentDate": "2026-09-04"
}

### An FT without a customer NIF -> 422 invoicing.document.invoice_needs_tax_number.
### An FS is allowed one without, which is most of a trade counter's morning.

### 6. Add a line. The SKU and description are copied from the catalogue as they
### are today and never refreshed - the document says what the customer was told.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "{{partId}}",
  "quantity": 4,
  "unitPrice": 18.40,
  "discountPercent": 5,
  "vatCategory": "NOR",
  "vatPercent": 23
}

### An exempt line without its legal basis -> 422 invoicing.vat.exempt_needs_reason.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "{{partId}}",
  "quantity": 1,
  "unitPrice": 10.00,
  "vatCategory": "ISE"
}

### The same line, done properly.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "{{partId}}",
  "quantity": 1,
  "unitPrice": 10.00,
  "vatCategory": "ISE",
  "exemptionCode": "M07",
  "exemptionReason": "Isento artigo 9.o do CIVA"
}

### 7. Issue it. Number, ATCUD, signature and QR code, all at once, inside one
### transaction holding a lock on the series. Nothing here can be undone.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/issue
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{}

### 8. Read it back. This is everything a printed page needs: the four printed
### characters of the signature, the QR payload, and the tax split by category.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### Add a line to it now -> 422 invoicing.document.already_issued.
### Issue it again -> the same. The signature covers the total.

### 9. Find it the way a customer quotes it down the phone.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/by-number/FT%20SERIE2026/1
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 10. The day's documents.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents?from=2026-09-04&to=2026-09-04
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### The drafts nobody has issued yet - the work in progress, not the record.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents?draftsOnly=true
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 11. Void it. It keeps its number, its figures, its signature and its place
### in the chain. Nothing is deleted, because the number was reported the moment
### it was issued and a missing number is worse than a cancelled one.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/void
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "reason": "Raised against the wrong customer account"
}

### Voiding without a reason -> 422 invoicing.document.void_reason_required.
### The reason goes into the SAF-T export, so there is no blank version of it.

### 12. Close the series at year end. One-way, and the AT has to be told.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/series/{{seriesId}}/close
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

###############################################################################
# SALES -> INVOICING
#
# The bridge runs in both directions and by two different mechanisms, which is
# the interesting part.
#
# Invoicing asks Sales a synchronous question, because it cannot draw a document
# without knowing what is on the order and whether the order is ready. Sales
# learns the answer by event, because it is only being told that something has
# already happened - and putting a second module inside the transaction that
# takes a document number is the one thing that transaction must not do.
###############################################################################

@orderId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### 1. The order has to have gone out first. Confirm it and dispatch every line.
###    Drawing from a confirmed-but-undispatched order
###    -> 422 invoicing.from_order.not_dispatched
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/from-sales-order
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "salesOrderId": "{{orderId}}"
}

### 2. Once it is fully dispatched, the same call produces a DRAFT - not an
###    issued document. Somebody should see what is about to go to the customer.
###    The type comes from the order: an account order is an FT, a counter sale
###    is an FR, because a counter sale is paid as it is handed over.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/from-sales-order
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "salesOrderId": "{{orderId}}",
  "documentDate": "2026-09-07"
}

### 3. Read the draft. The SKUs and descriptions are the ones the ORDER recorded,
###    not the catalogue as it stands today: a part renamed between the sale and
###    the invoice keeps the name it was sold under.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 4. Issue it as normal. Sales finds out through the outbox a moment later.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/issue
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{}

### 5. The order now knows. invoiceDocumentNumber and invoicedOn are populated.
GET {{host}}/api/sales/orders/{{orderId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### 6. Drawing a second document from it -> 409 invoicing.from_order.already_invoiced,
###    and the message names the document that already exists.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/from-sales-order
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "salesOrderId": "{{orderId}}"
}

### 7. Void the document. The order becomes billable again - the invoice keeps
###    its number and its place in the signature chain forever, but it no longer
###    bills anybody. Without this, an invoice raised against the wrong customer
###    would leave the order permanently unbillable.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/void
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "reason": "Raised against the wrong customer account"
}

### And now step 2 works again.

###############################################################################
# THE REFUSALS WORTH SEEING
###############################################################################

### An FT for a customer with no NIF on file
### -> 422 invoicing.from_order.customer_has_no_tax_number
### The law requires an invoice to identify its customer. Record their NIF in
### Partners, or sell it as a simplified invoice instead.

### A line priced at a rate this region does not use - say 21% on the mainland
### -> 422 invoicing.vat.percent_not_in_region
### The total would be right and the SAF-T heading would be wrong, which
### reconciles perfectly until somebody compares the VAT return with the file.

### A line at 0% -> 422 invoicing.vat.exempt_needs_reason
### Zero is not a rate, it is an exemption, and an exemption needs its M-code.
### That is the one thing an order genuinely cannot tell us, so the line has to
### be added by hand:
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/lines
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "partId": "{{partId}}",
  "quantity": 1,
  "unitPrice": 10.00,
  "vatCategory": "ISE",
  "exemptionCode": "M07",
  "exemptionReason": "Isento artigo 9.o do CIVA"
}

###############################################################################
# SAF-T (PT)
#
# The monthly filing. A rendering of documents that already exist, so it is a
# GET and asking for it twice gives the same bytes - which matters, because the
# first thing anybody does with a rejected file is fix something and ask again.
#
# Configure Erp:Invoicing:Company and Erp:Invoicing:Product first. Nothing else
# in the module needs them, so the application starts happily without them and
# only this refuses, naming the setting that is missing.
###############################################################################

### The month, which is the period the filing actually wants.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/saft/2026/9
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### An arbitrary period, for a check or a partial re-export.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/saft?from=2026-09-01&to=2026-09-30
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### A period crossing new year -> 422 invoicing.saft.period_spans_years.
### One file carries one fiscal year in its header; reporting December under the
### wrong year quietly is worse than refusing loudly.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/saft?from=2026-12-01&to=2027-01-31
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

### WHAT IS IN THE FILE, AND WHY
###
### TaxAccountingBasis is F - billing software, documents and no accounts. The
### ledger, the suppliers and the purchase documents are absent because they
### belong to modules this one cannot see, which is the same reason they are
### absent from the obligation.
###
### Drafts are excluded: no number, so they do not exist as far as the AT is
### concerned. Voided documents ARE included, with status A and their reason -
### the number was reported the moment it was issued, and leaving it out would
### put a gap in a sequence that is required to have none.
###
### Products are built from the document lines rather than from the catalogue.
### A part withdrawn or renamed since it was sold still has to appear exactly as
### the document names it, or the file references a product it never declares.
###
### Customers come from Partners, because the SAF-T customer table is explicitly
### a master file. Where Partners no longer knows somebody, the document's own
### snapshot stands in - a line references that identifier, and a file missing a
### customer it references is rejected as inconsistent. Missing address fields
### become "Desconhecido", which is the AT's own instruction rather than a shrug.

###############################################################################
# CREDIT NOTES
#
# The only correct way to reverse an invoice. The original stands exactly as it
# was - numbered, signed, and reported to the tax authority, none of which can
# be unsaid - and a new document cancels some or all of its effect.
#
# Voiding is the other remedy and a narrower one: for a document raised in error
# and caught before the customer acted on it. It leaves no record of money given
# back, which is why it is not what you reach for when they have already paid.
###############################################################################

@creditNoteId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### 0. A credit note needs its own series, registered for NC.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/series
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "type": "NC",
  "code": "NC2026",
  "year": 2026
}

### ... then its validation code and activation, exactly as for the FT series.

### 1. Credit the whole invoice. Omitting "lines" means everything still
### creditable, which is the common case: the lot came back, or it went to the
### wrong company.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/credit-note
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "reason": "Raised against the wrong customer account"
}

### 2. Or credit part of one line. The unit comes from the invoiced line - there
### is exactly one unit a line can be credited in, and asking for it again is
### only an opportunity to disagree.
POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{invoiceId}}/credit-note
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{
  "reason": "Four came back damaged",
  "lines": [
    { "lineId": "...", "quantity": 4 }
  ]
}

### 3. It is a draft like any other. Read it, then issue it into the NC series.
GET {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{creditNoteId}}
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

POST {{host}}/api/invoicing/documents/{{creditNoteId}}/issue
Content-Type: application/json
X-Tenant-Id: {{tenant}}

{}

### Issuing it into the FT series -> 422 invoicing.document.series_type_mismatch.
### A series is declared to the AT for one document type; putting a credit in the
### invoice series reports it as an invoice.

### 4. Crediting six more of a line where only four are left
### -> 422 invoicing.credit.exceeds_invoiced, and the message says how many.
### Crediting more than was charged is giving away money nobody ever paid.

### 5. The rest of a partly credited invoice can still be credited: omitting
### "lines" now means the four that are left, not the original ten.

### THE REFUSALS
###
### A draft                -> 422 invoicing.credit.original_not_issued
###                           Nobody has been given it, so there is nothing to
###                           credit back. Change the draft instead.
###
### A voided document      -> 422 invoicing.credit.original_voided
###                           It bills nobody. Crediting it would give money back
###                           against a demand already withdrawn.
###
### A credit note          -> 422 invoicing.credit.original_is_credit_note
###                           A credit raised in error is corrected with a debit
###                           note, which is the opposite instrument.
###
### Fully credited already -> 422 invoicing.credit.nothing_left
